    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "My research is on the ways in which young adults have come to learn about sex and\r\nsexuality specifically through parent/guardian teachings and formal sex education.\r\nThrough my work, I analyze interviews conducted with 25 undergraduates at Stanford\r\nUniversity on their memories of sex education, using their narratives to complement\r\nstatistics that surround teen pregnancy and STI rates in the US. I engage with social\r\ntheory on socialization, female oppression, and the performance of gender, as well as\r\nexisting literature on sex education and the state of reproductive health. The aim of this\r\npaper is to showcase the manner in which sex education plays a role in the perpetuation\r\nof problematic gender socialization binaries through misleading sex education practices.\r\nI demonstrate the benefit participant narratives can provide in illustrating more about\r\nthe human experience behind national reproductive health concerns, with the goal of\r\nproposing nuanced approaches to such issues.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
